{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>You shall surely send away the mother, and the young you shall take for yourself.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): Scripture commands the preservation of the species and not to eradicate it. For even though the Torah permitted the slaughter of living creatures for human benefit, it did not permit wanton destruction and eradication among them; and if one were to take the mother together with the young, it is as though he eradicated that entire species. And it says ״למען ייטב לך״ — that HaShem will show you compassion — ״והארכת ימים״ — and prolong your days — since you did not eradicate the whole species but left the mother and did not uproot the species. And when it says ״והאם רובצת על האפרוחים או על הביצים״ — 'and the mother brooding upon chicks or upon eggs' — Scripture thereby revealed to us that the eggs it mentioned are the young, and it called the eggs 'young' (בנים) because from them the young emerge. And from this we may conclude that one is not obligated in sending away the mother unless the eggs are intact, fit to become young; but if they are broken, he is not obligated in sending away. And it mentioned אפרוחים and ביצים in the plural, for such is the usage; and even if the mother is brooding upon a single chick or a single intact egg, he is obligated in sending away." "<b>And the Rambam</b>, of blessed memory, gave a reason for this commandment in chapter 48 of the Guide of the Perplexed: it is so that the mother not suffer distress at seeing her young taken; for if he took the young in the mother's presence, the mother would suffer anguish — similar to the commandment of אותו ואת בנו (slaughtering it and its offspring), that one may not slaughter the young in the presence of the mother. For the mother's love and compassion for the fruit of her womb does not follow the intellect but follows the activity of the imaginative faculty. And if concerning beasts and birds the Torah showed such compassion, how much more so concerning human children. If so, both this and that are in order that we distance ourselves from the trait of cruelty and conduct ourselves with the trait of mercy." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> ״שלח תשלח את האם״ — there is no commandment lighter among all the commandments than sending away the nest, and what is its reward? ״למען ייטב לך והארכת ימים״. To what may the matter be compared? To a king who hired laborers for his field to plant and did not disclose to them the wage for their planting. At evening, everyone who had planted one tree received one gold coin. They all began to wonder: 'This one planted only one tree, the lightest and least of tasks, and he gives him one gold coin — we who planted many, how much more so!' So too sending away the nest, in which there is no loss of money — its reward is thus; all the more so other commandments that involve loss of money and toil and saving of lives — how much more so! Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, did not specify to Israel the reward for the commandments, so that they would perform all of them. For thus we find that the commandments are likened to trees that produce fruit: one tree produces fruit more esteemed than another, and likewise one commandment has a great reward. And you already know that the commandment of hospitality — the fruit of its reward is children, and the proof is Sarah our mother, and also the Shunammite woman (II Kings 4) who received Elisha. And likewise there is a commandment whose reward is length of days, such as the commandment of honoring father and mother, and the commandment of sending away the nest. But in truth, the essence of 'length of days' refers to the World to Come. As it says (Isaiah 16:2): ״והיה כעוף נודד קן משולח תהיינה בנות מואב מעברות לארנון״. This is what Scripture says (Jeremiah 17:11): ״קורא דגר ולא ילד עושה עושר ולא במשפט בחצי ימיו יעזבנו ובאחריתו יהיה נבל״. What is written after it? ״כסא כבוד מרום מראשון מקום מקדשנו״. What has this to do with that? This 'caller' (קורא — a bird identified with the cuckoo) brings eggs from other birds and sits upon them until they emerge from their shells and become chicks, and they follow after him and pluck out his wings and eat him. When he wants to fly, he cannot, for his wings have been plucked; a beast or creeping thing finds him and eats him. What caused this? Because he stole eggs that were not his. So too the nations of the world stretched out their hands against the throne of glory, the place of our sanctuary — they destroyed and burned our Temple and exiled Israel. And the Moabites and Ammonites — the Holy One, blessed be He, will obliterate their memory, as it is written (Zephaniah 2:9): ״לכן חי אני נאם ה׳ צבאות כי מואב כסדום תהיה ובני עמון כעמורה ממשק חרול ומכרה מלח ושממה עד עולם שארית עמי יבזום ויתר גוי ינחלום״. Up to here is from the Midrash." "<b>And we find</b> that King Solomon equated these two commandments as one, as he said (Proverbs 30:17): ״עין תלעג לאב ותבוז ליקהת אם״. And they said in the Jerusalem Talmud, chapter 1 of Pe'ah: ״עין תלעג לאב״ — this refers to honoring father and mother; ״ותבוז ליקהת אם״ — this refers to sending away the nest. The Torah specified their reward, and Solomon came and specified their punishment." "<b>By way of reason (sekhel):</b> ״שלח תשלח את האם״ — the reason for the commandment is to teach us the trait of mercy and to distance us from cruelty, which is an evil disposition in the soul. Similar to what the Torah forbade (Leviticus 22:28) — to slaughter אותו ואת בנו (it and its offspring) on one day — and similar to what we were commanded in the Oral Torah, by way of mercy and not by way of cruelty, to slaughter by the neck and not from the back of the head. This is the view of the Rambam in the Guide of the Perplexed, which I already cited above." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah.</b> 'You shall surely send the mother' — she is Binah, from which the seven are drawn into every descent and every descent to renew the face of the earth, and she is the nest of the world, and is called the mother of the world. And so you will find in Sefer ha-Bahir: what is written, 'שלח תשלח את האם' — and it did not say 'the father,' but rather 'you shall surely send the mother,' in honor of her who is called the mother of the world, as it is written (Proverbs 2:3) ״כי אם לבינה תקרא״. And what is 'ואת הבנים תקח לך'? Rabbi Rehumai says: those children whom she raised, and what are they? The seven days of the festival and the seven days of the week, end of quotation. And this sending is only with a pure bird, and it is a service and an honor to her who is called the mother of the world, for it is impossible to take her and the attribute of 'day' does not apply to her, for she is above the seven days of Creation, which are not completed in number, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 147:5) ״ולתבונתו אין מספר״. And so the tenth is called 'mother,' in the manner of what is written (Exodus 20:12) ״כבד את אביך ואת אמך״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory said of (Proverbs 28:24) ״גוזל אביו ואמו״ — 'his father' is none other than the Holy One, blessed be He, and 'his mother' is none other than the congregation of Israel. And the reward in these two commandments is length of days, except that in the commandment of honoring father and mother it mentioned 'on the land,' and likewise (Deuteronomy 25) with the weights, but here in sending the nest it says למען ייטב לך והארכת ימים, and did not mention 'on the land.' And the meaning of והארכת ימים is that He lengthens even from the days, of which it is written (Exodus 31:17) ״כי ששת ימים״." "<b>And there is</b> another opinion, that in the fulfillment of this commandment there is an arousal of mercy upon the entire world. For in that he sends the mother, she is distressed and agonizes over the destruction of her nest and the distance of her children, and she goes on in sorrow and wants to destroy herself; and through her great distress the appointed prince in charge of the birds seeks mercy from the Holy One, blessed be He — and then the Holy One, blessed be He, of whom it is written (Psalms 145:9) ״ורחמיו על כל מעשיו״, draws the flow of mercy upon all who suffer and need mercy and has mercy upon them. You thus find that the one who fulfills this commandment and sends is a cause to arouse mercy upon the entire world, and it is good for him together with them, and this is what it says: למען ייטב לך והארכת ימים — that is, that sending will be for good to you, and the lengthening of days upon him, for length of days comes through the attribute of mercy." "<b>And I found</b> in the Midrash in Devarim Rabbah: the commandment of sending the nest — its reward is children. The Holy One, blessed be He, says: if you have no children, I give you children. From where? As it is said: 'שלח תשלח את האם ואת הבנים תקח לך' — 'you shall surely send the mother,' and what reward do you take? 'ואת הבנים תקח לך.' And this Midrash alludes to this mystery that I have mentioned. And its explanation is: that attribute is the place of the children; there Hannah stood with the intention of prayer when she asked for children, as it says (1 Samuel 1) ״ותתפלל על ה׳״ — there is the world that is entirely good, the world that is entirely long, as it is said (Psalms 37) ״והתענג על ה׳״; therefore He sealed this commandment with its reward, למען ייטב לך והארכת ימים. And they said in tractate Niddah, chapter 'Tinoket': What should a person do to become wise? Let him seek mercy from the One whose the wisdom is, as it is said (Proverbs 2:6) ״כי ה׳ יתן חכמה״. What should a person do to have children? Let him seek mercy from the One whose the children are, as it is said (Psalms 127:3) ״הנה נחלת ה׳ בנים״. And understand this teaching — how the Sages arranged these needs according to the order of the attributes. And this commandment is among the received commandments, and the Sages of blessed memory established a blessing for all of them but did not establish one for this one, because it is not an obligatory commandment like the rest of the commandments, and one is not required to pursue it deliberately but rather when it happens to present itself to him — and this is the language of כי יקרה; and furthermore, one is not obligated in sending unless he wants to take the children."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:47.644051008Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}